# Pre-edit safety copies. This project has no build step and edits happen in place,
# so a .bak-<pass-name> snapshot is taken before each editing pass. Useful locally for
# diffing a pass, but git history supersedes them — never commit one.
*.bak-*
*.bak

# Local dev server output. The preview is `python3 -m http.server`, which logs to stdout,
# but redirected logs land here often enough to be worth ignoring up front.
*.log
logs/

# EXCEPT ONE, AND IT IS AN EVIDENCE ARCHIVE RATHER THAN OUTPUT. .claude/refusals.log holds the runs
# where a gate in the check chain REFUSED — appended by .claude/run_checked.sh, staged by
# .claude/commit_checked.sh, declared in .claude/battery.py's NON_INSTRUMENTS. It is committed on
# purpose: a refusing run reaches no commit, so without this the chain archives its passes into
# commit messages forever and discards the runs that demonstrate a check can fire at all. The name
# is the ruling's word for it; this line is why the general rule above does not reach it.
!.claude/refusals.log

# macOS filesystem droppings — Finder metadata and resource-fork stubs.
#
# EVERY COMMENT IN THIS FILE MUST BE PROSE, two words or more. A single-token comment that loses its
# hash becomes an ignore pattern, and whatever it named silently stops being addable: no error, the
# path simply disappears from `git status`. This line used to read `# macOS`, which is exactly that
# shape. The rule holds whether or not anything is checking it, which is why it is stated here and
# not just enforced elsewhere.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*

# Editor scratch
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Python bytecode. Two .pyc files were committed by accident and then went stale, which
# leaves the working tree permanently dirty and masks real changes in `git status` — the
# signal several gates here read. The instruments are the .py files; the .pyc is a build
# artefact of running them.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
